\section{Separate Resolution from Invocation}

A dynamic loader can tell the runtime where an export lives without
telling it how to call the export. Symbol lookup and relocation
belong to the link/load layer \cite{levine2000linkers}; the runtime
still needs a target calling convention and a prototype to construct
the call. In the Common Lisp extract, foreign function definitions
include argument and result types and distinguish the Lisp binding
name from the foreign name. Keep that distinction in the runtime:
two public methods may wrap the same symbol with different policies,
but neither can invent a different machine signature for it.

For a running example use \texttt{double affine(double x, int n,
double y)}, defined to return \(x \cdot n+y\). On System V AMD64,
the integer and SSE argument classes draw from distinct register
sequences. Thus the logical second argument does not use the second
integer register, nor does the logical third argument use the third
floating-point register. This allocation follows the passing rules
in the local supplement, not a rule such as ``parameter \(k\) always
uses register \(k\).'' The same instruction set with a different
calling convention can assign this signature differently.

