\section{Give Each Stub a Machine Job and a Runtime Job}

There are three common ways to realize the machine job: emit a typed
C shim in advance, build a call with a target-aware invocation
library, or emit code from the runtime. A shim lets the C compiler
perform ABI classification, including obscure aggregate cases. A
dynamic call engine is convenient when signatures are discovered
at runtime. A code emitter can optimize repeated calls, but must
also handle stack alignment, register preservation, spill placement,
prologue and epilogue rules, and unwind metadata where applicable.
The first implementation should not hand-assemble calls just because
it can generate code for its own language. That code generator may
use a different convention for its internal calls.

The runtime job is to convert values, arrange roots and leases,
transition to a foreign state when appropriate, and restore the host
calling context. In a dynamic engine, the array of argument pointers
often points at aligned native argument cells. A pointer parameter
then has a cell containing the pointer, and its entry in the array
points at that cell, not at the pointee. The cells must be kept until
the invocation has finished. \Cref{fig:visual-387} marks this
distinction between preparing bytes and placing them into ABI
locations. The return cell is another machine value until the wrapper
converts it back.

