\section{Prepare a Reusable Stub Without Retaining Call Locals}

\begin{verbatim}
bind(module, spec):
    lease := retain_module(module)
    try:
        entry := resolve_export(lease, spec.export_name)
        shape := (spec.target, spec.abi, spec.native_signature)
        machine_plan := get_or_compile_call_plan(shape)
        return ForeignFunction(lease, entry, machine_plan, spec)
    on_error:
        release_module(lease)
        raise

invoke(bound, args):
    require_arity(bound.spec, args)
    with bound.borrow_module_for_call():
        with new_marshalling_frame() as frame:
            for i in argument_order(args):
                frame.stage(i, bound.spec.convert(i, args[i]))
            frame.seal()
            with foreign_execution(frame.roots):
                bound.plan.call(bound.entry, frame.argument_cells,
                                frame.return_cell)
            return bound.spec.decode(frame.return_cell)
\end{verbatim}

The shape key is sufficient only for a plan that takes the entry
point as data, and only if the signature encodes all lowering-relevant
facts. Include fixed versus variadic mode and the fixed count when
supporting both. A specialized stub that embeds an entry address
must also carry the module lifetime; caching it by symbol name would
permit an unload and reload to leave a stale address in executable
code. The call lease must be acquired atomically with checking that
the binding is still open. A concurrent close may prevent new
invocations but cannot unmap a module whose native frame is running.

The call frame is per invocation and owns conversion temporaries,
including any allocated before a later argument fails validation.
Keep it installed while decoding the result if that step can allocate
or throw. When an API reports errors through thread-local state,
that state must be captured immediately after native return, before
a runtime transition or other helper can overwrite it. Arrange
capture in the shim or machine plan, not in the eventual decoder.
Provide a dedicated policy when an API does not allow callbacks
during a region otherwise marked foreign.

\section{Compare Against Calls Emitted by the Target C Compiler}

Use a reference caller compiled against the real prototype and a
test library compiled separately. Try the mixed scalar example,
then signatures that exhaust integer registers before SSE registers
and the converse. For stack arguments choose distinct sentinels in
nonadjacent slots, not a run of equal zeros. Verify both the values
that arrived and the result consumed by the runtime. A simple test
returning the first argument may pass even if every following
argument is misplaced. Finally, force collection during result
boxing, and close the binding while a conforming fixture waits
inside native code. The first checks the runtime frame; the second
checks module ownership, which a successful register test does not
exercise.

\section{Account for a Hidden Result Parameter and Its Storage}

Extend the example to a native routine that returns a record
containing three plain doubles. On System V AMD64 this ordinary
24-byte aggregate classifies as MEMORY. The caller allocates
result storage and passes its address in the first integer
argument register. The called function writes there and
returns the same address in \texttt{rax}. For a signature
with a leading visible pointer, that visible pointer is no
longer in \texttt{rdi}; it moves to \texttt{rsi}. Visible
floating-point parameters still start their own SSE sequence.
In a C shim the compiler implements the convention; if
implementing the machine plan directly, compute the hidden
arguments before assigning visible locations.

Reserve return storage with the aggregate alignment and keep
it distinct from locations the callee can access through
other names. Moving the record into its host representation
can require allocations; the return storage remains valid
until conversion is over. An invocation engine with a
separate return-buffer parameter may already insert the hidden
pointer. Do not also append it to the public argument vector.
That would not produce two valid copies of one address: it
would shift the rest of the effective signature.

Build a fixture that produces three unrelated doubles based
on both its leading pointer argument and a separate numeric
argument. Initialize the return storage with distinguishable
defaults and make the pointed input a different allocation
whose contents must remain unchanged. Compare each result
field and the untouched input after the call. This fixture
tests caller allocation, hidden position and full-width
return consumption, in contrast to the earlier scalar
fixture which verifies only register assignment among
explicit parameters.

Exercise the error path with a failure during conversion of
the second argument. That failure must not call the callee,
and it must reclaim any first-argument native allocation
without reading or decoding the return storage. Counters
on native entry and allocator release make these two
expectations independent. A prepared machine plan has no
per-call cleanup to perform on this path because none of
the machine invocation has begun.
